.Dd Jul 10, 2025
.Dt RASM2 1
.Sh NAME
.Nm rasm2
.Nd Radare2 Assembler and Disassembler Tool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm rasm2
.Op Fl ACdDehHjLvwrNqxp
.Op Fl a Ar arch
.Op Fl b Ar bits
.Op Fl c Ar cpu
.Op Fl F Ar parser
.Op Fl k Ar kernel
.Op Fl o Ar ofile
.Op Fl s Ar addr
.Op Fl @ Ar addr
.Op Fl S Ar syntax
.Op Fl i Ar len
.Op Fl I Ar dir
.Op Fl l Ar len
.Op Ar code|hex|-
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Assembles and disassembles code or hexpair strings for multiple architectures and syntax formats.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Fl a Ar arch
Set architecture plugin
.It Fl A
Show analysis information of given hexpair string
.It Fl b Ar bits
Set architecture bits (8, 16, 32, 64)
.It Fl B
Binary input/output (\-l is mandatory for binary input)
.It Fl c Ar cpu
Select specific CPU (depends on \-a arch)
.It Fl C
Output in C format
.It Fl d
Disassemble hexpair bytes. rasm2 \-d 9090
.It Fl D
Disassemble showing hexpair and opcode
.It Fl e
Use big endian instead of little endian
.It Fl E
Output disassembled instructions in ESIL format
.It Fl f Ar file
Read data from file instead of ARG
.It Fl F Ar parser
Specify parse plugin to be used (pseudo)
.It Fl h
Show usage help message
.It Fl hh
Show long help message including supported assembler directives
.It Fl H Op var
Display variable
.It Fl i Ar len
Ignore/skip N bytes from the beginning of the input buffer
.It Fl I Ar dir
Add an include search directory for SPP (\-p). May be repeated; directories are
searched in command-line order after the input file's directory. Relative
\-I paths are resolved from the current working directory.
.It Fl j
Output in json format
.It Fl k Ar kernel
Select operating system (linux, windows, darwin, android, ios, ...)
.It Fl l Ar len
Input/Output length
.It Fl L Op name
List RArch plugins: (a=asm, d=disasm, e=esil, p=parse)
.It Fl LL Op name
List RAsm parse plugins
.It Fl N
Don't load any plugin, same as R2_NOPLUGINS=1 or r2 -N
.It Fl o Ar ofile
Output to file, for example 'rasm2 \-Bf a.asm'
.It Fl p
Run SPP over input for assembly
.Pp
Relative .include paths are resolved from the directory of the input file
specified with \-f, or the current directory for standard input and inline code.
Additional \-I directories and SPP_INCDIR are searched afterward. SPP_INCDIR
accepts a colon-separated list of directories (semicolon-separated on Windows).
Absolute include paths are used directly. Nested includes first search the
including file's directory; included assembly is preprocessed recursively.
Include names may be double quoted. Missing includes, .error, unbalanced
conditionals and excessive nesting fail assembly without emitting partial code.
.Pp
.set NAME VALUE stores a preprocessor environment variable; .get NAME expands
its value. .if NAME tests that variable (unset, empty, or a value starting with
0 is false), and .ifeq NAME VALUE compares it as a string. .else and .endif
control the selected branch. These conditions do not evaluate assembler .equ
symbols or numeric expressions: use .set TARGET_GB 1 followed by .if TARGET_GB.
Directives inside comments and quoted assembly strings are literal.
.It Fl q
Quiet output (handy for -L, -v, ...)
.It Fl r
Show output in r2 script
.It Fl s Ar addr
Define initial start/seek address (default 0)
.It Fl @ Ar addr
Alias for -s
.It Fl S Ar syntax
Select syntax output (intel, att, masm, ...)
.It Fl v
Show version information
.It Fl w
Describe opcode (what's op)
.It Fl x
Use hex dwords instead of hexpairs in the assembler output
.El
.Sh STRING DIRECTIVES
.Li .charset \(dqname\(dq
selects an RMuta encoder for subsequent string literals.
Supported encoders initially include pokered, ebcdic37 and base64.
.Li .charset \(dqraw\(dq
restores direct source-byte emission, the default at the start of each assembly.
Unknown names and plugins without strict encoding support fail the entire
assembly, even if no string follows.
Selection emits no bytes and is inherited by textually included source.
.Pp
.Li .ascii \(dqtext\(dq
emits the encoded literal without a terminator.
.Li .string \(dqtext\(dq ,
.Li .asciz \(dqtext\(dq
and
.Li .asciiz \(dqtext\(dq
append one raw zero byte after encoding.
Game-specific terminators must be emitted explicitly with a numeric
.Li .byte
directive.
.Pp
Strings must be double quoted.
Escapes are interpreted once before encoding; \exNN supplies an input byte
to the encoder.
Text encoders reject malformed UTF-8 and unmappable characters without
emitting partial output.
Embedded NUL is encoded or rejected explicitly, never treated as the end
of the input.
Base64 processes each complete literal independently, including its padding.
Numeric data directives, .hex, .incbin and instructions bypass charset encoding.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.charset "pokered"
\&.ascii "A<LINE>B"
\&.byte 0x50
\&.charset "ebcdic37"
\&.string "AB"
\&.charset "base64"
\&.string "AB"
\&.charset "raw"
.Ed
.Pp
This emits 804f8150c1c2005155493d00.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Ev R2_NOPLUGINS
Do not load shared plugins (speedup loading)
.It Ev R2_PLUGINS_ORDER
Plugin load order: i=internal, e=env, h=home, s=system; default=iehs; ? prints help
.It Ev R2_LOG_LEVEL
Change the log level
.It Ev R2_DEBUG
If defined, show error messages and crash signal
.It Ev R2_DEBUG_ASSERT
Enables runtime assertions for debugging
.It Ev RASM2_ARCH
Same as rasm2 -a
.It Ev RASM2_BITS
Same as rasm2 -b
.El
.Sh DIRECTIVES
.Pp
List the supported assembler and preprocessor directives with:
.Pp
 $ rasm2 -hh
.Bl -tag -width ".org address, fill"
.It Cm .byte Ar expression Ns Op , Ns Ar expression ...
Emit one byte for each comma-separated expression.
Spaces and tabs are allowed within expressions; commas are required between
operands, so whitespace-only lists are no longer accepted.
Each result must be in the range 0 through 255.
The
.Cm .db
and
.Cm .int8
directives use the same syntax and range.
For example,
.Cm .byte 1 + 2, (1 + 2) * 3
emits 0309.
.It Cm .dw Ar expression Ns Op , Ns Ar expression ...
Emit one value per comma-separated expression, honoring the endian.
The same list syntax applies to
.Cm .word ,
.Cm .short ,
.Cm .dd ,
.Cm .dword ,
.Cm .dq ,
.Cm .qword ,
.Cm .intN
and
.Cm .uintN .
For example,
.Cm .dw 0x1234, 0x5678
emits 34127856 in little endian.
An empty operand or an out-of-range value rejects the whole directive.
.It Cm .align Ar boundary Op , Ns Ar fill
Emit fill bytes until the current program counter is a multiple of boundary.
Without fill, the byte set by
.Cm .fillvalue
is used.
An already aligned PC, or a boundary of 0 or 1, emits nothing.
Alignment is relative to the PC, not to the file offset.
For example,
.Cm .byte 1; .align 4, 0xcc
emits 01cccccc.
.It Cm .fillvalue Ar byte
Set the default padding byte used by
.Cm .align
and by
.Cm .fill
when its value is omitted.
It must be in the range 0 through 255, starts at 0 for each assembly and
applies to the directives that follow it.
.Cm .org
without fill never emits bytes and ignores it.
.It Cm .org Ar address
Set the program counter without emitting bytes.
This preserves the historical
behavior, including moving backwards, and changes the addresses used for
labels and relative instructions without seeking in the output file.
.It Cm .org Ar address , Ar fill
Emit enough copies of the fill byte to advance the current program counter to
address.
The fill expression must evaluate to a byte (0 through 255).
The target uses the same address space as the current PC, including any
initial \-s address or preceding one-argument .org; it is not a file offset.
A target equal to the current PC emits nothing.
A backwards target, invalid
fill or padding size exceeding the assembler's output-size limit is an error.
Expressions must be resolvable when the directive is encountered.
.El
.Pp
The two-argument form provides GAS-style padding.
Unlike GAS, omitting fill
retains the PC-only behavior; use an explicit zero fill to emit zero padding.
For example, this emits 01ffffff02 and places the final byte at address 4:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rasm2 '.byte 1; .org 4, 0xff; .byte 2'
.Ed
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pp
Assemble opcode:
.Pp
  $ rasm2 \-a x86 \-b 32 'mov eax, 33'
.Pp
Disassemble opcode:
.Pp
  $ rasm2 \-d 90
.Pp
Show all supported architectures:
.Pp
  $ rasm2 -L
.Pp
Assemble with specific syntax:
.Pp
  $ rasm2 -a x86 -b 32 -S att 'mov eax, 33'
.Pp
Output in JSON format:
.Pp
  $ rasm2 -j -a x86 -b 32 'mov eax, 33'
.Pp
Use big endian:
.Pp
  $ rasm2 -e -a arm -b 32 'add r0, r0, r1'
.Pp
Disassemble and show ESIL output:
.Pp
  $ rasm2 -d -E 90
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Pp
